  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Discovering Key Omission**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed a crucial oversight: `CARD_TINT_OPTIONS` was absent from `src/types/theme.ts` during the compaction stage. Furthermore, the user\'s prompt emphasizes color consistency, specifically requesting that card colors remain faithful to the theme, using the theme\'s color with a subtle shade variation.\n\n\n**Refining Visual Consistency**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on visual consistency, focusing on the card\'s background and text visibility. The user\'s insistence on theme-adherence is clear, so I\'m concentrating on how to make the card backgrounds match the selected theme, while ensuring readability. Screenshots and design improvements in both light and dark mode are being considered, and the theme\'s colors need to be bold and clear.\n\n\n**Implementing Theme Harmony**\n\nI\'m now fully committed to visual harmony. I\'m ensuring all cards adhere strictly to the active theme\'s aesthetic (`themeId`), and I\'m using theme colors and tailored shades. For example, in light mode, I\'m defining specific color schemes, like the \"Coquette\" theme with its blush and berry tones or the \"Cyber Y2K\" theme with its icy periwinkle and electric indigo hues. I\'m focusing on background colors, borders, shadows, and text to match with the corresponding active theme.\n\n\n**Defining Theme Detail**\n\nI\'m now detailing theme-specific color palettes. \"Matcha Latte\" uses `#F6FAF4` for the app, and `#F0FDF4` for the card, with a `#15803D` border and shadow, and jet black text. \"Midnight Muse\" uses `#F8F6FC` for the app, and `#F5F0FF` for the card, with a `#6B21A8` border and shadow, and jet black text. I am also working on `CARD_TINT_OPTIONS` to support backward compatibility. I can fix the build error by exporting this variable, and cards stay true to their active themes.\n\n\n**Adjusting Dark Mode Contrast**\n\nI\'m now addressing the contrast issue in light mode for the dark/light mode switcher. The black icon on a black background was problematic. I\'m modifying the button to have a black pill with white text and a white moon icon in light mode, ensuring better visibility and adhering to the user\'s feedback.\n\n\n**Improving Dark Mode Visuals**\n\nI\'ve just refined the dark mode visuals, making them incredibly clear. I\'ve switched the light mode button to a black pill shape with white text and a white moon icon. The text is now readable as \"White Moon\" alongside a beautiful white moon icon. This is explicitly designed to enhance clarity in light mode. I have improved readability on the mobile header as well.\n\n\n**Enhancing Light Mode Clarity**\n\nI\'m now overhauling light mode text for improved visibility. I\'m swapping the grey text in `Navigation.tsx`, `App.tsx`, and `WritingCard.tsx` with a high-contrast black. This includes desktop navigation links, the offline badge, and mobile bottom navigation, filter tabs, format badges and even the input placeholder, ensuring consistent clarity throughout the interface. I\'m focusing on ensuring it\'s always high-contrast, bold, and black.\n\n\n**Solidifying Black Text**\n\nI\'m now making all text solid black in light mode to enhance legibility across the application. This includes story titles, author information, reading stats, comments, and buttons within the reader modal, as well as text in the writing editor and penpal hub. I\'m applying `text-black font-extrabold` where emphasis is needed, and `text-black` more generally to improve the look and feel of the app.\n\n\n**Refining Gen Z Aesthetics**\n\nI\'m now integrating Gen Z design elements. In light mode, I\'m using bold, theme-colored outlines and neobrutalist shadows for a tactile feel. I\'m focusing on soft pastel backgrounds that are theme-matched, moving away from plain white. For example, I\'m thinking of a Silk Bow stamp for Coquette, or a Chrome Starburst for Cyber Y2K. I\'m also ensuring all the texts are black, bold and high-contrast.\n\n\n"
