  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining AuthorNote Customization**\n\nI\'m currently working on enhancing the `AuthorNote` interface within `src/types/writing.ts` to enable personalized customization. Specifically, I\'m thinking about how to add support for target audience specification, to better tailor author notes. This is a crucial step for increasing the versatility of the writing tool.\n\n\n**Implementing AuthorNote Features**\n\nI\'ve been further developing the `AuthorNote` interface, specifically focusing on the new fields. I\'m considering a design where I can specify the target user with an ID or email, or use the value \"all\" for all users. I also want a custom name field for recipients, such as \"Clara Delacroix.\" Furthermore, I\'ve added a `themeStyle` property to align the author note appearance with the selected theme, and include an optional tag for categorization.\n\n\n**Defining AuthorNote Capabilities**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how the developer will interact with the new `AuthorNote` features. I\'m fleshing out the UI for composing notes. It\'ll include options for broad or targeted recipients, a title, the main message area, a byline, a category badge, and theme customization. Action buttons will dispatch the note immediately or preview it as a modal popup.\n\n\n**Designing Note Presentation**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the user experience after a note is triggered. I\'m focusing on an elegantly designed modal, `AuthorNoteModal.tsx`, which serves as a popup card. This modal triggers when applicable, undismissed notes exist. The design will be exceptional across all themes, with initial attention on \"Coquette Blush\". It will highlight note status, recipient info, and stats within a card-style UI. I\'m aiming for animations and seamless UI. The \"Deactivate / Archive Note\" options need consideration, alongside resetting dismissal.\n\n\n**Visualizing UI Theme Options**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into UI theming, and designing multiple visual options for the notes. I\'m imagining a \"Coquette Blush\" theme that includes a rich ruby rose border, a vintage wax seal and cursive accents. Next, there is a \"Cyber Y2K\" theme with a lavender-blue gradient, holographic starbursts, and a neobrutalist cyber shadow. Finally, I\'m considering a \"Matcha Latte\" theme with a creamy, botanical matcha foam and a ceremonial forest green border.\n\n\n**Visualizing New Theme Design**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the note themes. I envision a \"Midnight Muse\" theme, with a royal lavender gradient that transitions from light to dark mode. The design will include a cosmic vector seal, silver sparkles, and deep black ink typography. I also have an idea for a card feature: a \"Note from the Author\" badge, a personalized recipient display, and a signature section with the author\'s avatar.\n\n\n**Enhancing Visual Feedback**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the user experience for closing and reopening author notes. I\'m focusing on \"Fold Letter & Close\" and a top-right close button, aiming for smooth animations using `motion` and Tailwind transitions. I\'m implementing a backdrop fade and a scale/spring entrance/exit for the modal. To ensure easy re-access, I\'m considering a subtle floating \"Note from Author\" pill in the main feed, with an unread glow. Lastly, I\'m focusing on UI fixes for Tablet and Mobile views, especially in the image presentation.\n\n\n**Adjusting Responsive Layout**\n\nI\'m now refining the responsive design across different screen sizes. I\'ve tackled the navigation bar in `Navigation.tsx`, adjusting gaps and text sizes for a cleaner look on tablets. I\'m ensuring the \"New Piece\" button displays properly and the theme button is compact. For the feed control bar on tablets, I\'m implementing a clean flex-wrap to prevent horizontal overflow. Additionally, I\'m adapting the card grid on tablets to a 2-column layout and making sure the footer buttons remain compact and don\'t collide. Finally, I\'ve truncated the brand name text in the mobile header.\n\n\n"
