    thinking: "**Analyzing Code Structure**\n\nI\'m currently focusing on the code at line 162, specifically `const allUsers = StorageService.getAllUsers();`. After that, I\'m examining lines 573-618 in `DevAcademy.tsx` to understand the User Accounts Directory Table\'s rendering process. I am seeking to understand how the user data is handled and displayed.\n\n\n**Examining Directory Display**\n\nI\'m now diving into the \"User Accounts Directory\" section. Specifically, I am scrutinizing the table rendering process to trace the data flow from its source to its display. The structure seems straightforward, with the table head, and then a body section with the the data. I\'m focusing on lines 580-600.\n\n\n**Tracing Data Flow**\n\nI\'m now examining the loop that populates the user directory table. Specifically, I\'m focusing on how each user\'s data, including avatar, display name, email, and role, is rendered into table rows. I\'m taking a close look at how the `count` variable is calculated, and what conditional styling is applied based on the user\'s role. I\'m also scrutinizing the `key` attribute and the use of user IDs.\n\n\n**Implementing UI Consistency**\n\nI\'m now focused on applying the UI style from the writing log to other sections. My current task is to implement the card-based UI across the project, including the user accounts directory, Penpals Circle, sound features, widgets, and dev mode. I am focused on clean data display and theme integration when user data is opened, ensuring that the visual depth is consistent throughout.\n\n\n**Refining Account Directory Display**\n\nI\'m now meticulously designing the User Accounts Directory Card, focusing on a glassmorphism style container. I\'m incorporating a top accent bar, sheen overlay, and a decorative watermark to enhance visual appeal. The header will feature a badge showing the number of registered accounts and a search input. The user rows will be interactive cards with avatar, display name, email, penpal ID, and pieces count, along with a \"Inspect Full Dossier\" button.\n\n\n**Detailing User Data Modals**\n\nI\'m working on the user data modal\'s design and functionality when a user is clicked. I\'m focusing on the glassmorphism UI card structure, including the backdrop blur, frosted glass sheen, and a sticky header for crucial user data. The goal is a clear, concise display of user details within the modal body, including system user ID, full name, email, penpal ID, role, and creation date, all presented in a scrollable format. The \"Inspect Full Dossier\" button will also be removed, since it\'s redundant.\n\n\n**Expanding User Profile Details**\n\nI\'m now integrating detailed information into user profiles. I\'m incorporating the bio and writer manifesto, favorite beverage, and a literary quote with an elegant callout. I\'m also including the daily writing word goal. Additionally, I\'m working on a section to display their penpal network, connections count, and a list of authored writings with metrics like total pieces, words, stars, and draft/published counts. I\'m also adding a \"Read & Inspect\" button for each piece, which will open the content using `onOpenPiece(piece)`. Finally, there will be a \"Switch to & Login As This User\" action using `loginAs(user)`.\n\n\n"
